# 15. Key Learnings

By the end of Day 3, I learned:

1. How a processor executes an instruction.
2. The purpose of the Program Counter.
3. Why the PC normally increments by 4 in RV32I.
4. How an instruction is decoded.
5. How `rs1`, `rs2`, and `rd` select registers.
6. How the Register File reads and writes data.
7. Why RISC-V register x0 is always zero.
8. How the ALU performs arithmetic and logical operations.
9. Why an Immediate Generator is required.
10. How the different RISC-V instruction formats encode immediate values.
11. How RTL modules are verified using testbenches.
12. How VCD files are generated and inspected using GTKWave.

---

# 16. Day 3 Conclusion

Day 3 established the basic execution path of the RV32I processor.

The Instruction Decoder, Register File, and ALU were implemented and verified independently.

The basic execution concept can now be represented as:

```text
Instruction
     ↓
Decode
     ↓
Read Registers
     ↓
Execute
     ↓
Result
```

These modules will later be integrated with the Immediate Generator, Control Unit, Data Memory, Branch Unit, Program Counter, and other components to build the complete single-cycle RV32I processor.

